and now one of the political world's  most well-known  oracles the american pollster and  communications  strategist frank luntz is best known for  his analysis of american politics  he hit the headlines earlier this year  when he declared that he'd had enough of  donald trump and the republican party  but he's always retained a keen interest  in uk politics too  ever since advising boris johnson on his  campaign for the presidency of the  oxford union  35 years ago now luntz has undertaken  the survey of this nation for the centre  for policy studies  focusing on language and attitudes  towards political  and economic issues and is here to  discuss those findings ahead of the  survey's  full publication later this week frank  luds  what were you actually trying to  understand with this survey  well in three words populism woke-ism  and extremism i've seen the damage  that's done to american politics to the  american economy  and i want to see that happen here in  the uk i spent three years at oxford in  the 1980s  i was recently a lecturer for a week at  the vlavotnick institute at oxford  and i loved it i really do love it here  and i appreciate the civility and the  decency that people  exhibit i appreciate the respect that  they have for different points of view  and it's in essence a warning a wake-up  call  and and a road map for how to avoid  the mess that we've got in america right  now and i say this on the fourth of july  and it really disappoints me  because i still love my country and i'm  still  i'm still patriotic i still get  emotional when i see the flag  but i get emotional when i see your flag  as well the union jack  and i want to see understanding i want  to see respect i want to see civility  across the political spectrum  so that's why i'm here and that's the  purpose of the cps study  well we were talking earlier on the  program about that issue and we just had  a by-election i think you'll know here  where uh i have to say it wasn't  particularly  um characterized by civility but let's  have a look at the whole picture  let's start with what people think about  um  political leaders in britain you ask a  question about how political leaders  make people feel  look at that that's the number one  answer we gave them a list of 18  attributes and disappointed came in  first ignored irrelevant  the first eight words we gave them ten  negative words and eight positive words  and the first eight that they chose were  all negative  that this is a wake-up call for the  conservatives for lib dems for labor for  the greens it's a wake-up call for  everyone in politics  that the public isn't hearing you that  you may be  speaking you may be doing events you may  be sending them emails and text you may  have a website  but to them it's all noise you need to  cut through the clutter and i'm going to  give you three  recommendations for it number one is  that they want to know what you are for  not what you are against so don't define  yourself by the other party define  yourself  by yours number two the highest priority  right now  is honesty and trust so be candid with  them say what you mean and mean what you  say  and number three safety security  strong uh successful that the number one  focus  is still the economy but number two  right underneath it and right behind it  is crime  so give people a better quality of life  and they'll give you  a re-election that's um  well that's that's quite a big steer for  us as a program this  sounds like we'd um do better in ratings  if we had fewer politicians on  bit of a problem for us but the the  point you make there about division  which we again we discussed earlier on  um in the united states we see a lot of  division on the  issues of race and so on uh is that  reflected here what's the greatest  divide that we're seeing in britain  today  the number one divide is by wealth it's  by  income inequality we've got the same  challenge in the states even the  conservatives  who talk about economic freedom even  they  prioritize rich versus poor as  uh as the number two behind uh british  born versus  immigrant but overall the rich versus  poor  divine and by the way this is a warning  for the conservatives  that they need to understand and reflect  the public's feeling that nobody  succeeds  if everyone doesn't succeed that this  idea of every and all  is essential every person needs a shot  at success every person in every  community  needs a shot and a ladder up the  opportunity to succeed  it's not the definition of what happens  at the end that matters  they're not asking for equal outcomes  which is  a labor challenge but they are asking  for equal opportunity they're asking for  a healthy economy  an open economy and they're asking that  they be respected  as hard-working taxpayers that's  interesting  because um you know that's clear and  that's a traditional  divide in this country but we hear a lot  of noise about what  people these days call culture wars the  issues  of race of uh religion religion and  religious fundamentalism and all of that  and um this relatively new word which  other surveys recently here suggests  nobody really  or most people don't really know what it  means wokism what what  are we following america in our  obsession with that or is it something  it's not going to matter to us  i think it's going to matter deeply it  doesn't matter right now but it's coming  there are many ways that america follows  the uk  uh your vote from prexa was an  indication of a hidden vote  that that donald trump took advantage of  your vote for margaret thatcher back 40  years ago  was the leading indicator for ronald  reagan and there are times when america  is ahead of you such as bill clinton to  tony blair and right now  woke-ism cancel culture uh  the the language is mean and harsh  and the outcome will be negative as as  you've got right up there the  conservatives are deeply afraid of  rocism  labor is more afraid of racism even  though  third of your country does not know what  locism is it's still the number three  consumer  the number three ism and here's why they  know that it's divisive they know that  it's polarizing they know that it's not  being put  forward to have a better more unified  country which is what the  cps study is all about it's about unity  woke-ism is about division about  polarization about punishing people for  success  and not respecting hard work and  meritocracy and by the way i don't know  if you've got this data  but to my surprise people put equality  over meritocracy now i need to give a  proviso  inequality is unacceptable in 2021 uk  and 2021 u.s  equality a discrimination  that we expect everyone everywhere to  have that opportunity  and we want that opportunity to be  available to everyone based on hard work  creativity and outcome so  i would think that we would want to  cherish meritocracy but right now  equality is a higher priority to the  british population  one of the um that's interesting one of  the outputs of the  if you like this the concern about these  issues of workers and so on is  what's called cancel culture i people's  essentially having the right to free  expression deprived  um what what do we think about that we  think that freedom of speech is the  number one  freedom we believe that freedom of  speech freedom of expression  so nobody wants to cancel rallies nobody  wants to limit  protests it's a very very small  percentage  of the uk population but they want to  ensure  that those who work hard play by the  rules pay their  taxes that they're respected not  punished  and you'll see a big difference between  conservative voters and labor voters  on this issue of cancer culture and in  fact on every issue  we have a divide in america uh  personally and the way we treat each  other  that divide is not as great in the uk  however  the uk political policy divisions  priority divisions  are even wider than the us and i will  tell you from personal experience here  on the fourth of july on a day  that i should be celebrating by going to  fireworks and having burgers and  and uh coleslaw and and just celebrating  independence  it's very hard to celebrate because we  are so mean  and so ugly and so rotten to each other  the language that we would not  accept our kids using is language that  politicians use against each other every  day in america  and they don't want to see that in the  uk we've been conducting focus groups of  cps and  and the response is people are  respectful and decent  and disagree with each other in a  positive way that's the kind of politics  you want to practice  that's the kind of politics we should be  doing in america  and i want to ensure that that kind of  politics stays in the uk forever  uh i want you to say it on on  independence day but maybe the  the brits have something to teach the  americans about being nice and  and civil uh but by the way one one um  of the results which i thought was very  interesting  is that we don't just have a low opinion  of politicians but also to some extent  of british  uh of business leaders um and there's a  i think we've got a  result about what we think business  leaders ought to be doing  yes and the one thing that's a shock to  me is that  climate in this country is now universal  that this is the only country where  arguably more people on the right are  environmentalists  and are focused on issues of climate  than people on the left  it's not happening anywhere else in the  globe but it is happening here  this is the one area where universally  the british public says let's be  environmentally friendly  let's have a cleaner safer healthier  globe  let's put our environmental community  first  it's very impressive to see i thought  it's really interesting that actually  conservatives appear to be marginally  more concerned about business leaders  doing something on climate change than  uh labor supporters yeah but you know  where we got universal agreement  the business community should stay out  of politics  they want business to focus on business  focus on employees focus on  their employees not workers and by the  way they're no longer businesses they're  employers  because the british public is so much  more supportive of those  who who create jobs than they are of  that company itself  and they want customers they want to  focus  on on employees and not  on shareholders and what's interesting  is investor  is so much more supported than a  shareholder because a shareholder  is just about making money and investors  making a personal commitment  in that in that business and that  employer and in the future  so we have a whole business lexicon  that'll be helpful  to uh to those involved in the british  economy and believe me  they need to use it because right now  the business community also  has some credibility issues frank you  need to spend more time with us we are  basically so nice and lovely and polite  on this side of  the water um and be good to see more of  you  i don't want to go home i want to stay  here but uh i need to leave in a few  weeks but  thank you once again even your interview  on sky  is more civil than what we have in the  u.s so i appreciate it

